o independently do all this  stuff but  it's really like we want you to learn  the process how to work with the team  how to do the code reviews the way that  we do them  and to just you know go through the flow  of it and then  you know  it'll it'll kind of you'll get into the  groove where you don't feel like you  have to ask about every single thing but  in the the beginning of working at a new  place you should ask tons of questions  you shouldn't hide your questions from  your manager because you're afraid to  feel dumb that's like  tip number one is ask questions at the  beginning because no one expects you to  know things and yeah this tracks i've  been on five different ios teams and  while over time i was able to identify  that there were patterns to how things  were built and shipped and stuff like  that each of them did things a little  bit differently that i really needed to  pay attention to the process and how  things were done in each company to make  sure that i wasn't breaking things and  to be like an effective member of the  team not to mention all of these  companies are in like very different  parts of the industry but also all the  people that i worked with at each  company were very different and i need  to figure out how i can work with them  and asking questions i know that it  sounds scary to like sound dumb or  whatever but i think it really shows  that you're a curious person that you  have some humility and that you're  really eager to learn making the career  switch for myself like  there's a lot of times where the  imposter syndrome kind of settles in  right and so i'm just like okay you know  i'm afraid to ask the question but at  the same time you know i kind of have to  bite the ball and be like hey you know  what is this how how can i achieve this  because the reality is i i don't know  and especially to working with other  members on my team who've been doing  this for a while it's you know it  sometimes can be a little intimidating  because as much as i am an overthinker  right i'm always like okay well what if  i ask this well i don't want them to  think that i'm you know like uh the  bottom of the bar or anything but at the  same time like if i don't ask the  question i'm not gonna know i've  definitely had experiences where i went  weeks without asking what like something  meant i didn't really understand the  conversations that were happening around  me and then when i stopped for a second  i was like hey i don't get this and then  someone explained it to me then i was  actually able to participate in those  conversations and get what's going on  and most people are not gonna judge you  for this and if they do maybe don't go  to that person for questions but i trust  you there are gonna be so many people  who are willing to help and at the end  of the day it's okay to make mistakes  too allow yourself the grace that you're  not gonna have everything figured out in  the first week because trust me nobody  ever does while it's uncomfortable to be  in this phase of like being in training  i think it can really help to have the  mindset that you're actually exactly  where you're supposed to be like it's  literally your job to be in training  just  try to enjoy  the time where you don't have a ton of  projects and you don't have  a ton on your plate because that'll  never happen again at that company those  first few weeks that's the only time  where you have no responsibilities  except learning so just  focus on learning don't try to like  speed up and like you know jump past it  and start working and being productive  it's like you'll have the rest of your  career there to do those things i just  can't stress enough to  to just allow yourself to  to not well to allow yourself to be okay  with messing up  right because  i can't tell you how many times i've  been there like afraid to to make the  wrong step and instead like i end up  either not doing it or in the end it's  not even a big deal because my teammates  will be like or tim uh yeah my team  members will be like oh you know like oh  i've done that before or like oh you  know like um you know it's not a big  deal like it's it's okay and lastly i  want to recognize that a lot of us are  starting jobs in remote environments  which we're all still kind of getting  used to and starting at a job where  you're not going to like naturally bump  into your co-workers or have lunch with  them every day and talk about stuff  poses a challenge for how you're going  to build personal relationships with  your teammates i think just being a  remote that's just been a huge  challenge because even though it's nice  to just  you know be able to kind of roll out of  bed and be able to just kind of hop on  my computer or go to my stand-up or  whatever the case may be  you know not having that personal  interaction sometimes can be a little  difficult organically like when you were  at an in-person company  you're able to like meet people for  coffee and doing all these things to  like you get to know people outside of  like the meeting the work meeting and  now you don't have any time with people  outside of the work meeting i think if  you're in like a remote environment it's  really hard  to make remote friends with people at  work so try to bring your whole self and  like you know don't bring your like  linkedin version of yourself  to the office you know you can share a  little bit about who you are and like  that's kind of how you make friends i  found that like joining meetings early  or even just spending the first five  minutes just like talk about non-work  stuff or staying afterwards to do so or  just like finding time to just like  connect with your co-workers in some way  is really helpful of course do so when  it's appropriate but it's a great way to  make connections and a lot of people  recommend setting up these like  one-on-one meetings where it's just the  two of you for either 30 minutes or an  hour to not only get to know their work  and their role but get to know them as  human beings as people it definitely  takes a lot more effort to make those  connections now that remote work is more  common but i really do think that your  experience at a company is really like  made by the people that you work with  and so definitely worth getting to know  them so that's it for this video thank  you so much for watching thank you to  selena luis and aiden for letting me  interview you and sharing your  perspectives with everybody if you're  starting a new job in tech then let me  know in the comments down below what  makes you nervous what are you scared  about what's helped you to kind of calm  down your nerves and if you're  interested in learning why i went back  to software engineering then make sure  to subscribe to this channel because  that video is coming soon alright thanks  again for watching and i'll see you next  time bye
 

